      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No, framing this as a minor point or a discussion suggestion would be professionally damaging and would almost certainly lead to immediate rejection or retraction, harming your metrics far more than the delay does. You cannot ignore a fundamental statistical error that invalidates your primary p-value; claiming it is just \"Future work\" would be admit that your current data is scientifically unsound. To salvage publication, you must rigorously re-run the analysis with standard estimators, acknowledge the overconfidence in the original results transparently, and revise the manuscript before submission.\nThis is a hard no on the delay alone: unless a correct p-value supports the biological finding, the reviewers cannot accept the paper, and all other praise becomes irrelevant. Therefore, the most responsible path to preserve your journal's reputation is to fix the core error immediately rather than risking a retroactive correction later which would look unprofessional.",
